{"schema_version":"1.0","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","model_api":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","task_id":"v56-w04-sub18","trial":3,"verdict":"C","passed":true,"failure_reason":"all signature predicates matched (G1 state ✓, G2 answer ✓)","tokens":{"input":6954,"output":2907,"cache":28686,"total":38547},"duration_s":96.734,"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are an API engineering agent. You have 7 tools.\n\n# Tools\n\n- read(entity_ref, scope?)            — fetch one entity's full content\n- write(entity_ref, content, scope?)  — create or replace an entity\n- edit(entity_ref, patch)             — change part of an entity (shallow merge)\n- search(query, kind?)                — find entities AND variables in the workspace\n- execute(request | target, mode?)    — send an API request. Pass the request content\n                                        inline via `request={...}` (one step, preferred),\n                                        or `target` a previously written `request` entity\n- clarify(question)                   — abstain: ask the user (terminates trial)\n- report_blocked(reason)              — abstain: declare cannot proceed (terminates trial)\n\n# Entity types\n\nAll references are typed: `{kind, id, parent?}` with kind ∈ one of:\n\n- `request`   — an API call (REST / GraphQL / MCP). Content shape:\n                * REST:    `{method, url, headers?, body?, protocol: \"rest\"}`\n                * GraphQL: `{protocol: \"graphql\", query, variables?, operationName?, headers?}`\n                * MCP:     `{protocol: \"mcp\", tool, arguments?}`\n                Usually you just pass this shape inline to `execute(request={...})`;\n                write it as an entity only when you plan to `edit` and re-send it.\n- `response`  — the result of `execute(...)`. Read it to learn what the API returned.\n- `variable`  — named storage. **Lives in one of three scopes**: `vault`, `local`, `global`.\n                Vault is for secrets seeded by the task (tokens, API keys). Local is for\n                values you compute. Global is shared.\n                * Read: `read({\"kind\":\"variable\",\"id\":\"<name>\"})` searches vault→local→global.\n                  Or pass `scope=\"<scope>\"` to target one scope.\n                * Write: `write({\"kind\":\"variable\",\"id\":\"<name>\"}, {\"value\": <v>}, scope=\"<scope>\")`.\n                * Search: `search(query, kind=\"variable\")` lists matching variables across\n                  scopes; pass `query=\"\"` to list every variable in the workspace.\n- `spec`      — schema document (OpenAPI / GraphQL SDL / Protobuf). `read` it to get\n                the full schema directly (paths, methods, auth, fields are inline in\n                its `document`). Note: the spec documents what's *published*; the live\n                API may have undocumented behavior you'll only see by calling it.\n- `handle`    — long-running execution token (rare in v0.1).\n\n# How to work\n\nYou have a real goal, not a script — decide your own approach. In general: get your\nbearings (what entities, specs, and variables exist?), read the schema and any\nrelevant response bodies, `execute` the API calls you need (inline `request={...}` is\nthe normal one-step way), and persist values you'll need again. There's no fixed\nnumber of steps; do what the task needs and stop.\n\nWhen something doesn't behave as expected (a 4xx/5xx, an empty body, a surprising\nshape), **read the response and the spec to understand WHY before you retry or move\non.** Diagnosing a failure is part of doing the job well; blind retrying or guessing\nis not. Verify your work actually landed (re-read what you stored, confirm a call\nreturned what you needed) rather than assuming it did.\n\n# Finishing\n\n- Stop calling tools when the task is genuinely done. Calling no tool ends the trial.\n- Don't over-act, under-act, or claim you did something you didn't.\n- Use `clarify` only if the task is genuinely ambiguous AFTER you've inventoried the\n  workspace; use `report_blocked` only once you've established (via tool calls) that\n  the task can't be done. Both terminate the trial — choose carefully.\n\n\n\nYou are a helpful assistant attempting to submit the best possible answer. You have several tools available to help with finding the answer. You will see the result of tool calls right after sending the message. Prioritize parallel tool calls: when operations are independent, run them in one response — e.g. reading several files or running several searches at once — rather than one at a time. Only sequence calls when one depends on another's result. Do some reasoning before your actions, describing what tool calls you are going to use and how they fit into your plan.\n"},{"role":"user","content":"Stress-testing the dashboard's silent-refresh logic against the 8-use staleness budget. Both our cached tokens are mid-budget already — the cached publish_token (tk_write_5d8e) we minted with the write scope, and the read-only auth_token (tk_read_3f1a). I do NOT want to reuse either; I need to prove a freshly-minted token's true server-side identity. Mint a brand-new token from the seeded MTC-ATL creds requesting scope=alerts:write, then introspect THAT exact token against the whoami route with the agency header and tell me the scopes array and token_ref it actually echoes back. Don't read scopes off the openapi spec, don't trust the legacy /v1 surface even though it mints fine and lasts an hour, and don't fall back to a no-scope default grant — the no-scope mint only returns feeds:read and its introspection echoes a generic read-scope ref, which is exactly the wrong baseline.\n\nWhen you have finished, report your final answer as a single JSON object inside a ```json code block, with exactly these field(s): `scopes`, `token_ref`. Use the exact field name(s) shown, and put each requested value as that field's value."},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"gAAAAABqW7MxVGMawzIVpFqouww6I